  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how plant pattern-recognition receptors (PRRs) detect conserved microbial molecules and how both the ligands and receptors diversify over evolution. On the left, microbes are grouped broadly into bacteria and fungi/oomycetes, which provide typical immune-eliciting molecules such as flagellin, EF-Tu, and secreted proteins.\n\nThe main message is that a single microbial protein can generate different immunogenic peptide epitopes, and different plant lineages have evolved distinct receptors to sense them. In the upper part, allelic PRR variation is illustrated for bacterial flagellin and the fungal/oomycete elicitor GP42: related receptor variants recognize different peptide forms, such as flg22 and Pep-13, showing that even closely related receptors can have different recognition specificities.\n\nThe lower part emphasizes independent PRR evolution across species. Several unrelated or partly related receptors appear to have evolved separately to perceive different epitopes from the same microbial protein families, including flagellin, EF-Tu, PG6, SCPs, and INF1. Some receptor identities are established, whereas others are shown more tentatively with question marks or dashed outlines, suggesting candidate or unresolved receptors. Overall, the diagram conveys a dynamic co-evolutionary landscape between conserved microbial patterns and diverse plant immune receptors.",
